        <h1 class="lt-h1">Why Parents Trust Muzzle Sleep: One of<br>the Only Mouth Tapes Tested Across<br>500+ Chemicals</h1>

        <div class="lt-media">
          <img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0886/5542/9998/files/Frame_5_2.png?v=1766530050" alt="Hero image placeholder">
        </div>

        <p class="lt-p">Every parent wants their child to sleep better.</p>
        <p class="lt-p">But no parent should have to wonder if the tape going on their child’s face is hiding chemicals behind “eco-friendly” marketing words.</p>
        <p class="lt-p">That’s why Muzzle Sleep was created with a simple rule:</p>

        <div class="lt-highlight">If it touches your child’s skin all night, it must be unquestionably safe.</div>

        <p class="lt-p">And to ensure that, we went far beyond typical consumer testing — including screening our entire finished product across more than <b>500 chemicals,</b> medical-device irritation testing, and some of the most advanced PFAS panels available today.</p>
        <p class="lt-p">Here’s what makes Muzzle one of the cleanest, most carefully tested mouth tapes ever produced.</p>

        <h2 class="lt-h2">Not Bamboo. Not Rayon. Real Breathable<br>Fabric Parents Can Trust.</h2>

        <div class="lt-media lt-media-gap">
          <img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0886/5542/9998/files/Frame_5_3.png?v=1766595353" alt="Fabric illustration placeholder">
        </div>

        <p class="lt-p">Many tape brands market their material as “bamboo,” “bamboo silk,” or “organic bamboo fiber.” It sounds natural, but here’s what most parents don’t know:</p>

        <!-- FIX: in design there are multiple highlight strips here -->
        <div class="lt-highlight">“Bamboo fabric” is almost always rayon (also called viscose)</div>
        <p class="lt-p">— a synthetic fiber made through a chemical manufacturing process.
Even the U.S. Federal Trade Commission warns consumers that textiles advertised as “bamboo” are typically chemically processed rayon, not natural bamboo fiber.</p>
        <div class="lt-highlight diff-color">According to the FTC: “The manufacturing process … involves toxic chemicals, and the final fiber … is rayon.” (FTC Consumer Guidance)</div>

        {% comment %} <p class="lt-p">Even the U.S. Federal Trade Commission warns consumers that textiles advertised as “bamboo” are typically chemically processed rayon, not natural bamboo fiber.</p> {% endcomment %}

        <p class="lt-p">Why does that matter?</p>
        <p class="lt-p"><b>Rayon production often uses carbon disulfide and sulfuric acid.</b></p>
        <p class="lt-p">Two chemicals of concern in occupational health research.</p>

        <p class="lt-p">A review published through Harvard-affiliated environmental health channels highlighted that <b>carbon disulfide exposure in rayon manufacturing has been associated with neurological, cardiovascular, and reproductive effects in workers</b> — not in consumers, but in manufacturing environments.</p>

        <p class="lt-p">(We are <b>not</b> claiming any specific brand uses these chemicals — only that rayon/bamboo viscose production commonly involves them, as documented in textile chemistry literature.)</p>

        <p class="lt-p">This is why we refuse to use bamboo-rayon backings.</p>

        <h2 class="lt-h2">What We Use Instead: A Clean, Non<br>Plastic, Breathable Textile</h2>

        <!-- MISSING IN YOUR BUILD: fabric closeup image above the donut -->
        <div class="lt-media lt-media-gap">
          <img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0886/5542/9998/files/Frame_5_4.png?v=1766595481" alt="Fabric closeup placeholder">
        </div>

